<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What macOS reads out to the operator when the app first asks for a camera or
  a microphone. `cargo tauri build` merges this file into the Info.plist it
  generates, because it sits beside tauri.conf.json; nothing has to name it.

  Without these two keys the app does not get a refusal, it gets killed: macOS
  ends a process that touches a camera with no NSCameraUsageDescription in the
  bundle it belongs to, and the operator sees a source that will not go live
  and a mixer.log that stops mid sentence.

  The camera, the screen and the microphone are captured by plugin processes
  the mixer starts, not by this shell, and the strings still belong here: the
  permission is granted to the application bundle at the top of the process
  tree, and that is this one. Screen recording has no key of its own; macOS
  writes that prompt itself and only names the app.

  Plain sentences, not a legal notice. This is read by somebody with twenty
  minutes before a service who wants to know why a window is asking.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>GodwinMix shows your camera in the mixer, so you can put it on screen and send it to whatever you are streaming to.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>GodwinMix takes the sound from your microphone, your sound card or your mixing desk and mixes it into the programme.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
